[{:seon.task/name "the-page-says-regression"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.cost/judge` answers a cost proof's verdict, a closed map of schema :seon.cost/verdict (a new key must be added to it; it already carries :accepted and :waiver-fault), and `seon.cost/verdict-lines` prints it. Given the proof's estimate as its second argument, verdict-lines says REGRESSION when the verdict is not ok? and the estimate holds an :overrun (past `seon.cost/ratchet` x of the overrun recorded for its task), as seon.cost-test/an-open-overrun-is-accepted-until-it-regresses sets one up, and `seon.cost/explain` passes it. The page's verdict box, `seon.render.ui/verdict-box`, calls `(cost/verdict-lines v nil)` with no estimate (a render is handed the verdict alone), so the page never says REGRESSION where the agent's text does: two wordings of one verdict.

The change: `seon.cost/judge` puts the regression on the verdict (a key of :seon.cost/verdict) when an estimate's overrun is exceeded, and verdict-lines reads it from the verdict, so the text and the page say REGRESSION from one value and verdict-lines needs no estimate for it. A changed docstring stales the wiki sections that cite the var; a section still true is marked reviewed with my.doc/reviewed!. (An older change for this exists in commit 6a406af0ac, on jolt/seon-merges-agent-base and not on main; main's seon.cost has changed since, with the waivers.)

Proof: seon.cost-test/a-regression-is-in-the-verdict (the verdict of an exceeded overrun carries the regression; verdict-lines says REGRESSION from the verdict alone; an accepted overrun carries none)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/a-regression-is-in-the-verdict}}
 {:seon.task/name "cost-tolerances-are-derived"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.cost/slack` {:ticks 3.0 :ns 3.0 :bytes 1.25 :calls 1.0} (bytes called exact, yet 25% from when the unit table flaked, 11.6 vs 8.9 KB, since fixed: seon.cost-test/a-unit-measures-the-same-across-runs; ns 3x for load), `seon.cost/steeper-by` 0.3 and `seon.cost/ratchet` 1.5 are guesses; test/seon/profile_test.clj asserts within 2x and only when contracts are on (zero assertions when off). Correct: each unit entry records its spread over k runs under a declared cache state; a dimension's slack is the spread of the units its estimate prices (bytes then near 1.0); ns carries the run's load and its spread at that load; the exponent tolerance is derived per dimension and proof as log(slack_d)/log(n_max/n_min) (over 100x: bytes 1.25 -> 0.048, ns 3 -> 0.24); the ratchet is the overrun's own measured spread at filing; every verdict prints the derived tolerances, and the agent's prompt renders them from seon.cost; profile_test asserts the sampling rule exactly (ceil(n/64) timed calls) with contracts on. Tried: jolt/unitflake (2838e62..cc43b5b; retargeted diff next/the archived paper worktrees/unitflake-retargeted-2026-10-22.diff (git history), applies on 5af7b4b); not merged because on main it turns three green cost proofs red, so the derivation is wrong: the calls exponent tolerance derives to 0.000 (every unit entry's calls range is exactly 1.0) and a fixed per-call overhead bends a linear fit (`seon.doc-cost-test/an-unchanged-reload-costs-a-parse-and-a-pull-per-section` n^1.01, `seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass` n^1.03), and the ratchet tightened to 1.26x puts `seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program` past its open overrun. The plan owes: an exponent tolerance that holds for exact dimensions (from the fit's residual, or the estimate's fixed term), the ratchet's rule stated, then the unit table re-measured with seon.cost-test/table (:pr-str/char measures 33.05 B against the entry's 29.36)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-exponent-tolerance-follows-slack-and-span
                      seon.cost-test/slack-is-the-units-measured-spread
                      seon.ctx.wire-test/the-prompt-states-the-judges-own-tolerances}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-db-function-draws-its-cases-from-a-domain-generator"
  :seon.task/plan "Follow-up of seon.cost/a-contract-carries-its-cost-bound. ``:seon.host/db`` is a predicate, so malli generates no db, and a generated cost proof of a db-taking function (```seon.ctx/build```, ```seon.ctx/patch```, ```seon.ctx.wire/render```) has no cases. The lab (docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract) drew sessions of a shape (evals, runs, cited vars) over a seeded db, and the bounds composed from those shapes predicted the measured cost at 0.83 to 1.08x on 25 random shapes. A domain generator per input kind is the one source of a db function's cases, and its measures name what the bound may read. The lab's caveat: a1's own measured request was 6x over the shape-only bound, because a1 holds related search over vectors, proof sources and big values that the generator does not vary. Proof: build's generated proof against a bound in session measures, red when the bound leaves out a measure the generator varies."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/generated-cost-proof-seon-ctx-build-runs-cases}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-growth-fit-holds-whatever-ran-before"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-01 (batch 75's bin/prove --merge): seon.program-cost-test/a-change-re-folds-only-what-reaches-it went red in its shard (ticks grew as n^1.52; linear allows n^1.53 over [30 100 300]) and green alone, on a tree whose change reaches no code it measures. Cause: `seon.cost/growth` fitted increments over the smallest size, and at 100 the increment was 35k ticks on 373k at 30 (9%), so a few thousand ticks of process history (caches warm or cold from the tests the shard ran first) in one size moved the fitted exponent across `steeper-by`. What exists now: growth fits increments only when the largest increment is at least `seon.cost/growth-share` of the smallest size's cost and each fitted one at least `seon.cost/increment-floor`, else it judges the total or a bounded fixed part. That narrows the class but is a share of the smallest cost, not the measured spread of the process's history. Correct, in general: a proof's verdict is the same whatever its process ran before. Either the measure settles each size (seon.cost already runs a size until :seon.config.proofs/cost-settled: check that it settles the smallest size against a warm-up of other work), or `seon.cost/growth` refuses an increments fit whose largest increment is within the measured spread of the smallest size, and says so. Proof: a cost test run after a heavy unrelated workload in the same process gives the same verdict as run alone, for every cost proof whose fit sits within 0.1 of its allowance (list them with seon.cost/explain)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/cost-proof-fit-sits-within-0-1-allowance}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-page-proof-draws-the-task-it-says-it-draws"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-05 on jolt/simpler-schema. seon.render-cost-test/drawing-latest-costs-one-request and the wire cost proof of a request (the test named seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/reading-a-request-back-draws-each-turns-parts-once no longer exists; its counterpart is seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-requests-cost-is-its-records) name a task part in their :work, but their inputs' agent has no :seon.agent/task (render_cost_test's `latest-of` says so in a comment; wire_cost_test's `session-of` makes agent b/a1 with none), so the page they measure draws no task. Linked at the branch's tip the render proof measured 4,135 calls against 3,920 (and the wire one 1,586 against 566, 2026-11-05). Correct: the inputs link the task, as an agent's page does, and the bounds are set from that measure. A bound that rises is the owner's to approve (docs/cost.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-cost-test/drawing-latest-costs-one-request seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-requests-cost-is-its-records}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-functions-least-cost-accumulates-from-use"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-11: \"I wanted results to accumulate and become more precise over time naturally as they are exercised in the system. Tests, normal evals, normal system usage. That's the measure I care more about.\" What exists: a measure is one call, and only a size whose call is over its estimate is measured again (```seon.cost/run```, docs/cost.md#how-we-measure); 19 of 127 cost proofs need the warm calls, since a function's first call fills what later ones read, and each test runs in a fresh child, so a least kept across test runs stays the cold number. Warm calls happen in agents and root, where ```seon.profile/counted``` already counts every contracted call, but its Chez cell keeps per-epoch sums, no least and no input size. Correct (the data model agreed with the Data model session, notes data-model-design-2026-11-11.md section 5): the counter cell gains a least of one call (ticks, bytes) beside its sums; ```seon.profile/accrue``` carries it into :seon.fn/stats as two more members of the map, which gains the digest of the function's form it was measured at and resets when that moves, as :seon.test/digest does; a size bucket, when the function's :seon.cost/bound supplies a size of its arguments, is a key inside that map. A cost proof or the contract's bound then judges the accumulated least, and an over is a class of seon.problems' report, its task the fact. Cost: two compares a counted call (the cost proof of counted states it). Design and numbers: notes cost-measures-as-a-system-2026-11-11.md. Proof: a function called warm in an eval has its least in :seon.fn/stats at its digest, below its first call's cost, and reset when its form moves."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/a-functions-least-accumulates-from-its-calls}}]
